      "writtenOn": "2026-05-21T16:39:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In bootrom.cc (SendBootromCommandAes), raw pointers and pointer arithmetic are used to slice up the AES-GCM response:\n\n```c\n// Old:\nuint8_t *rsp_iv \u003d ret.data() + kStatusHeaderSize + kWrappedCommandSize;\nuint8_t *rsp_tag \u003d rsp_iv + kAesGcmIvSize;\nuint8_t *rsp_payload \u003d rsp_tag + kAesGcmTagSize;\n\n```\n\n**The Risk:** Pointer math completely circumvents bounds checking and makes off-by-one errors easy to introduce. **The Fix:** You already have std::span ret\\_span \\\u003d ret; declared just above this block. Use subspan() to safely carve out the regions:\n\n```c\n// New:\nsize_t offset \u003d kStatusHeaderSize + kWrappedCommandSize;\nauto rsp_iv \u003d ret_span.subspan(offset, kAesGcmIvSize);\noffset +\u003d kAesGcmIvSize;\nauto rsp_tag \u003d ret_span.subspan(offset, kAesGcmTagSize);\noffset +\u003d kAesGcmTagSize;\nauto rsp_payload \u003d ret_span.subspan(offset, rsp_wrapped_len);\n\n```\n\n*(Note: Be sure to update the AesGcmDecryptOpenssl call below it to accept these spans directly instead of reconstructing spans from pointers).*",
