      "writtenOn": "2026-05-21T16:27:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In bootrom.cc, BootromComm::SendBootromCommandAes() performs a bounds check on the response length:\n\n```c\nif (rsp_wrapped_len + kScHeaderSizeAes \u003e ret.size()) {\n  rsp_wrapped_len \u003d ret.size() - kScHeaderSizeAes;\n}\n\n```\n\nIf a malicious or corrupted bootrom returns an extremely large rsp\\_wrapped\\_len (e.g., 0xFFFFFFF0), the addition can wrap around on 32-bit host architectures. This bypasses the condition and leads to out-of-bounds memory access during AES-GCM decryption.\n\n**Remediation:** Rewrite the condition using subtraction to completely avoid overflow risk:\n\n```c\nif (rsp_wrapped_len \u003e ret.size() - kScHeaderSizeAes) {\n  rsp_wrapped_len \u003d ret.size() - kScHeaderSizeAes;\n}\n\n```",
