      "message": "In usb\\_comm.h, the USB device handle is managed as a raw pointer:\n\n```c\n// Old:\nlibusb_device_handle *handle_ \u003d nullptr;\n\n```\n\n**The Risk:** While UsbComm encapsulates this handle and calls libusb\\_close(handle\\_) in its destructor, manual resource management violates the \"Rule of Zero\". If exceptions were ever thrown from UsbComm, or if the class was improperly moved, the handle could leak. **The Fix:** You already correctly wrapped the libusb\\_context in main() using a custom-deleter unique\\_ptr. Apply the exact same pattern to the handle.\n\n```c\n// New (in usb_comm.h):\n#include \u003cmemory\u003e\n\nclass UsbComm {\n  // ...\n private:\n  std::unique_ptr\u003clibusb_device_handle, decltype(\u0026libusb_close)\u003e handle_{nullptr, \u0026libusb_close};\n};\n\n```\n\nWhen connecting in usb\\_comm.cc, you would reset it:\n\n```c\nhandle_.reset(libusb_open_device_with_vid_pid(ctx_, vid_, pid_));\n\n```\n\n*(This allows you to completely remove libusb\\_close(handle\\_) from the destructor and error paths, as it will clean itself up automatically).*",
